  "result": "Neither: start with **one agent** (a model that picks tool calls in a loop until the job is done). More agents don't always give better results.\n\nSay a student asks, \"Is the laser cutter free Friday, and does the manual allow 6 mm acrylic?\"\n\n1. **One agent** checks the calendar, searches the manual, then answers.\n2. **Split up**, a coordinator agent hands those jobs to calendar and manual agents.\n3. Each handoff adds model calls and can lose details, such as which machine was meant.\n4. You pay per **token** (a chunk of text the model reads or writes). Illustratively:\n\n```\none agent: 3 calls × 2,000 tokens = 6,000\nsplit:     7 calls × 2,000 tokens = 14,000\n```\n\nSplitting can pay off when large subtasks are independent, need separate context (the text a model sees), or can run in parallel. Test one agent first, and split only if the results show a problem that splitting addresses.\n\nWhich real request do you think might be too much for one agent, and why?",
